 I think to start, it's as easy and as intuitive as just iMessage or another general platform.

4798
06:03:54,080 --> 06:03:56,080
 You can just talk to your bots.

4799
06:03:56,080 --> 06:03:59,080
 And I think more importantly, bots are always on.

4800
06:03:59,080 --> 06:04:02,080
 And so you don't have to walk around with your laptop open anymore.

4801
06:04:02,080 --> 06:04:03,080
 You can actually close it.

4802
06:04:03,080 --> 06:04:07,080
 I can go on coffee walks and continue prompting from my phone.

4803
06:04:07,080 --> 06:04:17,080
 Or I can set it up on a long running task and then trust that it will actually complete that task end to end and do the thing without me having to kind of object in between and guide it.

4804
06:04:17,080 --> 06:04:20,080
 It really is the confidence to go carry out and do things.

4805
06:04:20,080 --> 06:04:26,080
 And there's many ways you can actually improve that by recording your own screen and some other stuff that I'll show more in the demo.

4806
06:04:26,080 --> 06:04:29,080
 But again, it also uses the tools just like you do.

4807
06:04:29,080 --> 06:04:35,080
 And so you can think of your basic connectors of MCPs, of APIs, everything that you've already kind of set up.

4808
06:04:35,080 --> 06:04:39,080
 But beyond that, what's really important is it actually owns its own VM.

4809
06:04:39,080 --> 06:04:41,080
 And so you can log into the browser.

4810
06:04:41,080 --> 06:04:48,080
 And for the parts of your SDR work that are a little more difficult or there's no real way to automate them, you can actually show bot in the browser.

4811
06:04:48,080 --> 06:04:50,080
 You can sign in to those tools.

4812
06:04:50,080 --> 06:04:54,080
 And you can actually show bot by recording your screen, recording you go through it.

4813
06:04:54,080 --> 06:04:56,080
 You can show the exact workflows that you do in there.

4814
06:04:56,080 --> 06:05:02,080
 The really tedious and specific stuff that's kind of hard to explain or maybe there's no real like open API for it.

4815
06:05:02,080 --> 06:05:06,080
 And so this is really something that I found immense value in.

4816
06:05:06,080 --> 06:05:12,080
 And what's given me the confidence to be able to say, okay, grok bot, you can genuinely go end to end with what you're doing here.

4817
06:05:12,080 --> 06:05:17,080
 And as I talked about before, I think shareable templates are one of the most important things.

4818
06:05:17,080 --> 06:05:28,080
 And I think every single team at a team level should basically all be spending time building their own templates, sharing those with the team, getting an understanding of, okay, what are the things that are actually working?

4819
06:05:28,080 --> 06:05:45,080
 And how can I not only just import that template, but how can I import it and then add my own nuance onto that of whatever like my tonality and my messaging is, or actually maybe it's just I like to do my workflow a slightly different way, but actually kind of taking this as a base template and then working beyond that.

4820
06:05:45,080 --> 06:05:50,080
 And you can also prompt grok bot directly and ask for direct improvements.

4821
06:05:50,080 --> 06:05:54,080
 So you can import templates and be like, hey, look at this template my colleague shared with me.

4822
06:05:54,080 --> 06:06:02,080
 What do you think are things with my own workflow that you think would be a good addition to build on top of this or to kind of get more value out of that.

4823
06:06:02,080 --> 06:06:10,080
 And so I really emphasize just actually like talking back and forth with your grok bots and kind of like starting to really push it to help you.

4824
06:06:10,080 --> 06:06:18,080
 And I think this is why we emphasize so heavily that grok bot is really just a multiplier for your own work and really a way to scale the outputs you're doing.

4825
06:06:18,080 --> 06:06:22,080
 And as an SDR, I don't think there's anything more important than actually doing that.

